-- Borrowing base - lending against a pool of collateral without ever
-- letting the loan outgrow what that collateral safely supports.
--
-- A quick tour of the domain, in case asset-backed lending is not your
-- world. A lender opens a revolving *facility* for a borrower and agrees
-- to advance money against a pool of pledged collateral - receivables,
-- inventory, whatever the borrower can post. One number governs how much
-- may be borrowed: the *advance rate*, the fraction of the eligible
-- collateral the lender will actually lend against - 80%, say, never the
-- full value, so there is always a cushion. As collateral is pledged and
-- money is drawn, the rule must keep holding: the total drawn can never
-- exceed the advance rate times the collateral. Months later an examiner
-- asks: on the day of this drawdown, was the facility within its advance
-- limit? The answer should be one lookup, not a spreadsheet dig.
--
-- The thing this example adds to the others is the *arithmetic* such a
-- rule needs. "At most 80% of the collateral" is a multiplication; "what
-- fraction has been drawn?" is a division. Earlier examples only added
-- and subtracted; here a rule multiplies, and a reporting view divides.
--
-- Here is how Morpholog thinks about it. It does not store bare "facts" -
-- it stores *claims* it has agreed to admit because they obey the rules.
-- Two kinds of rule do the work below, and the split is the usual lesson:
--
--   * an `invariant` is a rule that must hold for all admitted state,
--     forever. The runtime refuses to commit any change that would break
--     one - the total drawn can never exceed the advance rate times the
--     eligible collateral. A drawdown that would breach the limit is
--     rejected outright, with nothing recorded.
--   * a `require` is a gate on a single action, checked the moment you
--     act. Here the gates are the plain operational preconditions - the
--     facility exists, this drawdown id is new - while the proportional
--     limit lives in the invariant, so no path can leave the books over
--     the line.
--
-- A note on the arithmetic. The advance-limit rule is written in
-- multiplied form - `drawn <= advance_rate * base` rather than
-- `drawn / base <= advance_rate` - so the admission decision stays exact,
-- with no rounding. Division appears only in the read-only reporting view
-- at the bottom, where a rounded utilisation percentage is exactly what a
-- human reading a report wants.

program borrowing_base

-- The vocabulary. `Facility` carries the advance rate, set when the
-- facility opens. `EligibleCollateral` is one pledged asset and its
-- value. `Drawdown` is one advance of money against the facility.
-- One facility per id: the id determines the advance rate, so the
-- advance-limit rule below always reads one unambiguous rate. And one
-- pledge per asset: an asset backs exactly one facility at one value,
-- so a question about an asset always has one answer.
predicate Facility(facility: Subject, advance_rate: Decimal)
    unique by (facility)
predicate EligibleCollateral(facility: Subject, asset: Subject, collateral_value: Decimal)
    unique by (asset)
predicate Drawdown(facility: Subject, draw_id: Subject, amount: Decimal)
-- Read-only reporting views (see the derived claims at the bottom of
-- the file): each facility's drawn-to-collateral utilisation, and the
-- collateral register by asset.
predicate FacilityUtilisation(facility: Subject, utilisation: Decimal)
predicate AssetValue(asset: Subject, figure: Decimal)

-- Intents are messages the system sends to the outside world after a
-- change commits. The drawdown intent is how a downstream treasury system
-- learns it should actually move the money; Morpholog records only that
-- the drawdown is within the facility's limit.
intent FacilityOpened(facility: Subject)
intent CollateralPledged(facility: Subject, asset: Subject)
intent DrawdownRequested(facility: Subject, draw_id: Subject, amount: Decimal)


-- The rules that must always hold. Any change that would leave the world
-- violating one of these is rejected outright. Read `implies` as
-- "whenever the left is true, the right must be too".


-- The values have to make business sense, and keeping them sane is what
-- lets the rules below mean what they say. An advance rate is a fraction
-- of the whole - between 0% and 100%. Collateral is worth something
-- strictly positive: a zero or negative pledge is meaningless, and a zero
-- collateral base is exactly what the utilisation view at the bottom
-- would divide by. A drawdown moves money out, never in - modelling
-- repayments would be a separate action this example leaves out.
invariant advance_rate_within_unit_interval:
    Facility(facility, rate) implies 0 <= rate <= 1

invariant collateral_value_is_positive:
    EligibleCollateral(facility, _, v) implies 0 < v

invariant drawdown_amount_is_non_negative:
    Drawdown(facility, _, amount) implies 0 <= amount

-- The advance limit. The total drawn against a facility can never exceed
-- its advance rate times the value of its eligible collateral. Written in
-- multiplied form, so the check is exact: `drawn <= rate * base` rather
-- than a rounded `drawn / base <= rate`.
invariant outstanding_within_advance_limit:
    Facility(facility, advance_rate)
    implies sum(d | Drawdown(facility, _, d)) <= advance_rate * sum(v | EligibleCollateral(facility, _, v))


-- The only ways state may change. A transformation proposes a set of
-- changes; the runtime applies them only if every invariant still holds
-- once they are in place.

-- Open a facility, fixing its advance rate. The gate refuses a second
-- facility under the same id.
transformation open_facility(facility, advance_rate):
    require not Facility(facility, _)
    admit Facility(facility, advance_rate)
    emit FacilityOpened(facility)

-- Pledge an eligible asset to the facility. The gate insists the facility
-- exists; the asset and its value go on the record, raising the base the
-- advance limit is measured against.
transformation pledge_collateral(facility, asset, collateral_value):
    require Facility(facility, _)
    admit EligibleCollateral(facility, asset, collateral_value)
    emit CollateralPledged(facility, asset)

-- Draw money against the facility. The gates are operational only - the
-- facility exists, and this drawdown id has not been used. Whether the
-- new total stays within the advance limit is the invariant's job, so a
-- drawdown that would breach the limit is rejected with nothing recorded.
transformation draw(facility, draw_id, amount):
    require Facility(facility, _)
    require not Drawdown(facility, draw_id, _)
    admit Drawdown(facility, draw_id, amount)
    emit DrawdownRequested(facility, draw_id, amount)


-- A reporting view: each facility's utilisation, the fraction of its
-- pledged collateral that has been drawn. This is a *derived* claim -
-- read-only, recomputed on demand, never stored - so it can never drift
-- from the drawdowns and collateral it is built from. It is the one place
-- division is used: a rounded fraction is what a human reading a report
-- wants, where the admission rule above stays exact in multiplied form.
-- Defined over facilities that have pledged collateral, and every
-- collateral value is positive (the invariant above), so the base it
-- divides by is never zero.
derived FacilityUtilisation(facility):
    over EligibleCollateral(facility, _, _)
    value utilisation = sum(d | Drawdown(facility, _, d)) / sum(v | EligibleCollateral(facility, _, v))

-- The collateral register, by asset. An examiner's question is "what is
-- this asset on the books for?" - the asset identifies the row, and the
-- facility it happens to back is a detail the row does not need to
-- carry. So the view is keyed by the asset alone, even though the
-- records behind it also name a facility: a row is keyed by what
-- identifies it, not by everything it joins through. The figure is
-- picked out of the pledge record by NAMING the field wanted -
-- `collateral_value: _` marks it - with `..` leaving the facility
-- unstated. One pledge per asset (declared above) is what makes "the
-- pledge record for this asset" a single record to read.
derived AssetValue(asset):
    over EligibleCollateral(facility, asset, _)
    value figure = value EligibleCollateral(asset: asset, collateral_value: _, ..)
